# Europe batch 4 (1853-1927): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"eu185372": dict(
 bluf="Britain and France fought Russia to stop it taking Constantinople and the Straits and dominating the eastern Mediterranean. They won, at a cost of about half a million dead, most of them from disease. The war broke the international system established at the Congress of Vienna, and the wars of Italian and German unification followed within fifteen years.",
 now="The war ended the Concert of Europe: the powers that had kept the peace since 1815 fought each other, Austria's neutrality left it isolated, and the wars of Italian and German unification followed within fifteen years. Russia's defeat forced the emancipation of its serfs.",
 story="Russian Tsar Nicholas I occupied the Ottoman principalities of Moldavia and Wallachia in July 1853, and the Ottomans declared war in October. Britain and France joined in March 1854 after a Russian squadron destroyed an Ottoman flotilla at Sinope. Allied armies landed in Crimea in September 1854 and besieged Russia's Black Sea base at Sevastopol, which fell in September 1855. Supply failures and disease killed far more men than bullets; British nurse Florence Nightingale reorganised the hospitals at Scutari.",
 why="Russia wanted the Ottoman Balkans and the Straits. Britain and France wanted Russia kept out of the Mediterranean. A quarrel between Russia and France over the protection of Christians in the Holy Land supplied the occasion.",
 trig="Russia's occupation of the Ottoman Danubian principalities, Moldavia and Wallachia, in 1853.",
 then="The Treaty of Paris of 1856 banned warships from the Black Sea. Russian Tsar Alexander II freed the serfs in 1861 and rebuilt the army. Austria's neutrality cost it Russia's friendship, and it stood alone when Piedmont attacked in 1859 and Prussia in 1866.",
 fact="The first war covered by telegraph and photography; it gave Britain the Charge of the Light Brigade and Florence Nightingale's reform of nursing."),
"eu185973": dict(
 bluf="English naturalist Charles Darwin set out to explain how species arise from evidence gathered over twenty years, and published when Alfred Russel Wallace reached the same idea. He showed that variation, competition and inheritance produce new species over long periods, with no design required. Biology got its organising theory, religious accounts of creation lost their standing among the educated, and a misapplied 'survival of the fittest' served racism and empire.",
 now="Evolution is the foundation of modern biology and medicine, from vaccines to genetics.",
 story="English naturalist Charles Darwin had worked on the problem since the Beagle voyage, for more than twenty years. In 1858 naturalist Alfred Russel Wallace sent him a paper setting out the same theory. Darwin published 'On the Origin of Species' in November 1859. He argued that individuals vary, more are born than can survive, and those with helpful traits leave more offspring, so species change over long periods.",
 why="Darwin had the evidence for decades and feared the reaction. Wallace's independent discovery of the same theory in 1858 forced his hand.",
 then="Religious leaders attacked the book at once. Most scientists accepted evolution within decades; natural selection as the mechanism stayed disputed until genetics confirmed it in the 1930s and 1940s. 'Survival of the fittest' was applied to societies and races and fed eugenics and imperial ideology.",
 fact="The first printing of 1,250 copies sold out to the trade on the day of publication."),
"d-oxforddebate": dict(
 bluf="Bishop of Oxford Samuel Wilberforce attacked Darwin's book at the British Association meeting to defend scripture and the fixity of species; biologist Thomas Huxley answered to establish that science owed the Church no deference. Neither side won on the day, and the accounts that made it a rout were written years later. The legend outlived the exchange, and it marked the point at which English scientists stopped answering to bishops.",
 now="The retold debate set the rule in England that a scientific claim answers to evidence rather than to bishops; the professional, secular science of the later 1800s was built on that standing.",
 story="At the British Association meeting in the Oxford University Museum on 30 June 1860, Bishop of Oxford Samuel Wilberforce spoke against Darwin's book. He is reported to have asked biologist Thomas Huxley whether it was through his grandfather or his grandmother that he claimed descent from an ape. Huxley is said to have replied that he would rather have an ape for a grandfather than a man who used his gifts to obscure the truth. No full record of the exchange was taken.",
 why="Darwin's book, published seven months earlier, had provoked argument across educated England. Wilberforce had been briefed by anatomist Richard Owen, Huxley's rival.",
 then="Most scientists accepted evolution within about 20 years. Natural selection as its mechanism stayed disputed until the 1930s.",
 fact="Royal Navy officer Robert FitzRoy, Darwin's captain on the Beagle, stood up in the audience holding a Bible and begged the room to believe God rather than man."),
"eu186174": dict(
 bluf="Piedmontese Prime Minister Camillo Cavour set out to unite northern Italy under the House of Savoy with French help; nationalist general Giuseppe Garibaldi's volunteers took the south and handed it over. A Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed in March 1861 and counted as a great power in name. A richer north absorbed a poorer south in two years, and the divide between them has shaped Italian politics ever since.",
 now="Italy's north-south economic divide dates from unification and still shapes its politics, from regional parties to arguments over how taxes are shared.",
 story="Piedmontese Prime Minister Camillo Cavour won French help to drive Austria out of Lombardy in 1859. Nationalist general Giuseppe Garibaldi landed in Sicily with about a thousand volunteers in May 1860 and took Sicily and Naples by the autumn. Plebiscites joined the central and southern states to Piedmont. On 17 March 1861 Piedmontese King Victor Emmanuel II was proclaimed King of Italy.",
 why="Nationalism had a following among the educated and in the towns. Cavour's Piedmont had the army and the diplomacy. Austria stood alone after its neutrality in the Crimean War.",
 then="Venice joined in 1866 and Rome in 1870, after French troops left; the papacy refused to recognise the Italian state until 1929. The south rose in a brigands' war that the army put down through the 1860s.",
 fact="At unification only a small minority of Italians, by some estimates under 10 per cent, spoke standard Italian."),
"eu186174b": dict(
 bluf="Russian Tsar Alexander II freed 23 million privately owned serfs to modernise a state defeated in Crimea before revolt did it for him. Legal bondage ended; the peasants paid for their allotments over 49 years and often got less land than they had farmed. Rural poverty and land hunger fed the unrest of 1905 and 1917.",
 now="Emancipation left peasants paying for land they had farmed as their own; the land question it left open drove rural revolt in 1905 and 1917, and it remains the standing case for whether reform from above can hold without political freedom.",
 story="Defeat in the Crimean War convinced Russian Tsar Alexander II that Russia needed to modernise. His Emancipation Manifesto of 3 March 1861, 19 February in the Russian calendar, freed the privately owned serfs. The peasants received allotments and paid redemption dues for them over 49 years; the plots were often smaller than the land they had farmed before.",
 why="Military defeat came first. Fear of revolt came second: 'better to abolish serfdom from above than wait for it to abolish itself from below', the tsar told the Moscow nobility in 1856.",
 then="Redemption payments, debt and land hunger kept the villages poor. Peasant unrest ran through the decades and erupted in 1905 and 1917; the payments were cancelled in 1907.",
 fact="Many peasants refused to believe the terms and held that the true decree, giving them the land free, had been hidden by the landlords."),
"eu186775": dict(
 bluf="Habsburg Emperor Franz Joseph, defeated by Prussia and shut out of Germany, bought Hungary's loyalty with self-government. The Compromise of 1867 made a dual monarchy with two parliaments under one crown, army and foreign policy. Hungary was satisfied; the Czechs, Croats, Romanians and other Slavs were left without equal standing, and their claims pulled the empire apart in 1918.",
 now="The Compromise gave Hungary equal standing and left the Slavs of both halves without it, a structure that could satisfy neither nationalism nor reform; its collapse in 1918 drew the modern map of Central Europe.",
 story="Defeated by Prussia in 1866 and shut out of Germany, Habsburg Emperor Franz Joseph struck a deal with Hungarian leaders Ferenc Deák and Gyula Andrássy, the Compromise of 1867. The empire became Austria-Hungary: two states with their own parliaments and governments, sharing a monarch, an army, a foreign policy and the finances to pay for them. Franz Joseph was crowned King of Hungary in Buda on 8 June 1867.",
 why="Defeat by Prussia in 1866 left Austria weak. Hungarian nationalists demanded autonomy as the price of their loyalty.",
 then="The Czechs demanded the same standing and were refused in 1871. Croatia received limited autonomy under Hungary in 1868. The Slav question and the Magyarisation of Hungary's minorities ran to 1918.",
 fact="The economic terms of the Compromise came up for renewal every ten years, and the two halves fought over the shared budget at each renewal."),
"d-ems": dict(
 bluf="Prussian Minister-President Otto von Bismarck wanted a war that France would declare, so that the south German states would join Prussia and the other powers would stand aside. He cut the king's report of a meeting with the French ambassador so it read as a snub and gave it to the press. France declared war within six days, the south joined Prussia, and Germany was united within six months.",
 now="The edited telegram provoked France into declaring war on Prussia with the south German states on Prussia's side, the condition Bismarck needed for unification.",
 story="At the spa town of Ems on 13 July 1870 French ambassador Vincent Benedetti asked Prussian King Wilhelm I to promise that no Hohenzollern would ever again be a candidate for the Spanish throne. Wilhelm declined with courtesy and sent Prussian Minister-President Otto von Bismarck a report by telegram. Bismarck cut the report so that it read as a snub on both sides and released it to the press that evening. France declared war on 19 July.",
 why="Bismarck wanted a war with France to bring the south German states into a united Germany. France's government wanted a public victory over Prussia after the Spanish candidacy had been withdrawn.",
 then="The south German states joined Prussia. French Emperor Napoleon III was captured at Sedan on 2 September, and the German Empire was proclaimed in January 1871.",
 fact="Bismarck said the shortened text would have the effect of a red rag on the Gallic bull."),
"d-sedan": dict(
 bluf="Prussian chief of staff Helmuth von Moltke wanted to destroy the French field army before it could fall back on Paris. He encircled Marshal Patrice de MacMahon's army at Sedan and took it whole, with French Emperor Napoleon III inside. The Second Empire fell within two days, and every European army copied the railway mobilisation and general-staff planning that had done it.",
 now="The defeat gave birth to the Third Republic, and the loss of Alsace-Lorraine became a lasting grievance that fed French support for war in 1914.",
 story="On 1 September 1870 German armies under Prussian chief of staff Helmuth von Moltke encircled French Marshal Patrice de MacMahon's army at Sedan near the Belgian border. French Emperor Napoleon III, who was with the army, surrendered the next day with about 100,000 men. On 4 September crowds in Paris proclaimed a republic.",
 why="Germany mobilised faster by railway and planned better through its general staff. Its steel breech-loading artillery outgunned the French. French commanders hesitated into encirclement.",
 then="Paris was besieged from 19 September. Germany was united under the Prussian king in January 1871, and Paris surrendered the same month.",
 fact="Napoleon III went into exile in England and died at Chislehurst in Kent in 1873."),
"eu187076": dict(
 bluf="Prussian Minister-President Otto von Bismarck wanted a war that would bring the south German states into a Prussian-led Germany; French Emperor Napoleon III fought to keep Prussia from dominating the continent. Prussia won in six weeks of field battles and a four-month siege of Paris. A united German Empire replaced France as the strongest power on the continent, and the loss of Alsace-Lorraine kept France looking for allies and for revenge until 1914.",
 now="French resentment and German power shaped Europe's alliances until 1914, and the Franco-German rivalry ended only with European integration after 1945.",
 story="In July 1870 France declared war on Prussia after Prussian Minister-President Otto von Bismarck released the edited Ems telegram. Prussia and its south German allies mobilised fast by railway, encircled one French army at Metz and captured French Emperor Napoleon III with another at Sedan in September. Paris was besieged and surrendered in January 1871.",
 why="Bismarck wanted a war with France to bring the south German states into a Prussian-led Germany. Napoleon III wanted prestige and took a Prussian candidate for the Spanish throne as a threat.",
 trig="Bismarck's release of the edited Ems telegram on 13 July 1870.",
 then="The German Empire was proclaimed in January 1871. France lost Alsace-Lorraine and paid an indemnity of five billion francs; Napoleon III's empire fell and the Third Republic was born. Italy took Rome as French troops left.",
 fact="The French chassepot rifle outranged the Prussian needle gun; Prussia won with its steel Krupp artillery."),
"eu187177": dict(
 bluf="Prussian Minister-President Otto von Bismarck wanted the German princes to offer the imperial crown to Prussian King Wilhelm I while the victory over France was fresh. They did, in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles, and a united Germany under Prussian leadership came into being. The continent now held a single state stronger than any neighbour, and the alliances built to contain or to use it set up 1914.",
 now="A united Germany with the largest army and soon the largest economy on the continent replaced the balance of 1815 with a problem every other power needed to answer; the alliance systems of 1879 to 1907 were the answers.",
 story="With Paris under siege, the German princes proclaimed Prussian King Wilhelm I German Emperor in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles on 18 January 1871. France gave up Alsace and much of Lorraine and paid an indemnity of five billion francs.",
 why="Prussia's victories over Denmark in 1864, Austria in 1866 and France in 1870 let Prussian Minister-President Otto von Bismarck unite Germany on Prussia's terms.",
 then="Germany became continental Europe's strongest economy and army. Bismarck spent twenty years building alliances to keep France isolated and reassure everyone else.",
 fact="Germany proclaimed its empire inside the French palace of Versailles, a deliberate humiliation France repaid in the same hall in 1919."),
"eu187177b": dict(
 bluf="Paris radicals and the National Guard set up their own government to defend the republic and the city against a conservative national assembly they expected to restore the monarchy. The French army, under the government at Versailles, retook Paris in a week in May and killed perhaps 10,000 to 20,000 Communards. The Third Republic was secured as a conservative state, and the socialist left gained its founding example and its lesson that a revolution must hold the army.",
 now="The Commune's suppression killed about 20,000 and settled the Third Republic as a conservative state; its example gave the socialist movement a model of workers' government and a lesson, drawn by Lenin, that a revolution must hold the army.",
 story="After France's defeat and the siege, the government under French head of state Adolphe Thiers tried to seize the National Guard's cannon on 18 March 1871 and lost the city. Paris elected the Commune, which separated church and state and banned night work in bakeries. In the 'Bloody Week' of 21 to 28 May government troops retook the city street by street and killed perhaps 10,000 or more Communards.",
 why="Humiliation in the war and the hardships of the siege came first. Anger at a conservative national government that had moved to Versailles came second.",
 then="France stayed a republic, the Third Republic. Its left and right stayed divided by the memory of the week.",
 fact="German writer Karl Marx, in London, hailed it as the first workers' government."),
"eu187878": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Otto von Bismarck called the powers to Berlin to cut back Russia's gains from its war with the Ottomans before Britain and Austria-Hungary fought over them. The congress shrank the Russian-sponsored Bulgaria, recognised Serbia, Romania and Montenegro as independent and gave Austria-Hungary Bosnia to occupy. Every Balkan nationalism left dissatisfied, Russia felt cheated by Germany, and the quarrels ran to Sarajevo in 1914.",
 now="It set up the tensions that exploded in Sarajevo in 1914, and Bosnia's place between Serbs, Croats and Bosniaks remains unresolved.",
 story="Russia beat the Ottomans in 1877-78 and imposed the Treaty of San Stefano, which created a large Bulgaria. German Chancellor Otto von Bismarck hosted the powers in Berlin in June and July 1878. Bulgaria was cut back; Serbia, Romania and Montenegro became independent; Austria-Hungary occupied Bosnia; Britain took Cyprus.",
 why="Russia's victory over the Ottomans had created a large Bulgaria under Russian influence, which alarmed Britain and Austria-Hungary.",
 then="Serbian anger at Austria-Hungary's control of Bosnia festered, and Austria annexed it outright in 1908. Russia blamed Germany for its losses, and Bismarck's Dual Alliance with Austria followed in 1879.",
 fact="Bismarck called himself the 'honest broker' of the congress; Russia left convinced he had brokered for Austria."),
"eu188279": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Otto von Bismarck wanted France isolated and Austria-Hungary covered; Italy wanted a counter to France after losing Tunisia. The three signed a secret defensive alliance in May 1882 and renewed it to 1914. France answered with Russia in 1894 and Britain in 1904, and the two armed camps turned the Balkan crisis of 1914 into a general war.",
 now="It is the standard example of rigid alliance blocs turning a local crisis into a general war, a lesson still cited whenever alliances are debated.",
 story="In May 1882 Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy signed a secret defensive alliance. Each promised help if another was attacked by two great powers, and Germany and Italy pledged to aid each other against a French attack.",
 why="German Chancellor Otto von Bismarck wanted to keep France isolated. Italy was angry that France had taken Tunisia in 1881.",
 then="France answered by allying with Russia in 1894 and reaching an entente with Britain in 1904. Italy, never fully committed, switched sides in 1915.",
 fact="Italy attached a declaration that the alliance could never be directed against Britain, and in 1915 it joined Britain's side."),
"d-bismarck1890": dict(
 bluf="German Kaiser Wilhelm II, 31 and twenty months on the throne, wanted to govern in his own name and forced Chancellor Otto von Bismarck out. Bismarck's alliance system, which had kept France isolated through contradictions only he managed, lapsed within months when Berlin dropped the Reinsurance Treaty with Russia. Russia allied with France in 1894, and Germany spent the next generation planning the two-front war that resulted.",
 now="It is the clearest modern case of a structural shift caused by a personnel decision, and a standing argument against systems that depend on one person.",
 story="German Kaiser Wilhelm II, 31 and on the throne for twenty months, demanded Chancellor Otto von Bismarck's resignation on 18 March 1890 after disputes over social policy and the chancellor's right to see ministers alone. Three months later Berlin let the secret Reinsurance Treaty with Russia lapse. Russia turned to France; the Franco-Russian alliance was signed in 1894.",
 why="The alliance system rested on contradictions only Bismarck managed: Germany was allied to Austria and secretly pledged to Russia at once. Wilhelm II wanted to govern himself and had been told the Russian treaty was disloyal to Austria.",
 trig="Wilhelm demanded Bismarck's resignation on 18 March 1890; in June Berlin let the Reinsurance Treaty with Russia lapse.",
 then="Russia turned to France and the two signed a military alliance in 1894. German planning for a two-front war became the Schlieffen Plan.",
 fact="Punch marked the dismissal with the cartoon 'Dropping the Pilot': Bismarck climbing down a ship's ladder while the young Kaiser watches from the rail."),
"eu189481": dict(
 bluf="France wanted out of the isolation Bismarck had built around it; Russia wanted loans and a counterweight to Germany after Berlin let the Reinsurance Treaty lapse. A secret military convention agreed in 1892 and ratified in 1894 bound each to fight Germany if it attacked the other. Germany now faced war on two fronts, and the plan it drew to win one, through Belgium, brought Britain in when it was used in 1914.",
 now="That plan's march through neutral Belgium brought Britain into World War I, a lesson in how military plans can drive political decisions.",
 story="After Germany let its Reinsurance Treaty with Russia lapse in 1890, France offered Russia loans and friendship. A secret military convention, agreed in 1892 and ratified in 1894, pledged each to fight Germany if it attacked the other.",
 why="Germany let its Reinsurance Treaty with Russia lapse in 1890. France, seeking to end its own isolation, offered Russia loans and an alliance.",
 then="Germany now faced possible war on two fronts and planned for it with the Schlieffen Plan: beat France fast, then turn on Russia.",
 fact="French savers lent Russia the money that built its railways; by 1914 about a quarter of French foreign investment was in Russia."),
"jp-treatyrev": dict(
 bluf="Japanese Foreign Minister Mutsu Munemitsu set out to end the unequal treaties that put foreigners in Japan beyond Japanese courts; Britain wanted a modernising partner against Russia. The Anglo-Japanese Treaty of Commerce and Navigation of July 1894 ended extraterritoriality from 1899, and the other powers followed. Japan became the first non-Western state to leave the unequal treaty system, and went to war with China two weeks later confident of Britain's goodwill.",
 now="Treaty revision made Japan the first non-Western state admitted to the European order as a legal equal, which licensed its own imperial expansion from 1895 and its alliance with Britain in 1902.",
 story="Japanese Foreign Minister Mutsu Munemitsu negotiated the Anglo-Japanese Treaty of Commerce and Navigation, signed in London on 16 July 1894. It agreed that foreigners in Japan would face Japanese courts from 1899. The other powers followed.",
 why="Japan had a constitution and modern legal codes. Britain wanted Japan as a partner against Russia.",
 then="Two weeks later Japan went to war with China, confident of Britain's goodwill. Japan recovered full control of its tariffs in 1911.",
 fact="Japan had built the Rokumeikan, a Western-style ballroom in Tokyo, in 1883 to show foreign diplomats that it was civilised by their standards."),
"eu189882": dict(
 bluf="German Kaiser Wilhelm II and Admiral Alfred von Tirpitz set out to build a battle fleet strong enough to make Britain treat Germany as a world power. The Navy Laws of 1898 and 1900 began it; Britain answered with Dreadnought in 1906 and outbuilt Germany. The race ended Britain's isolation, pushing it to settle with France in 1904 and Russia in 1907, and made Anglo-German hostility a fixed condition before 1914.",
 now="The Anglo-German naval race is a standard example of a security dilemma and is often cited in debates over China's naval build-up and US power in the Pacific.",
 story="German Kaiser Wilhelm II and Admiral Alfred von Tirpitz pushed Navy Laws through the Reichstag in 1898 and 1900 to build a battle fleet that could challenge Britain's. Britain answered with HMS Dreadnought in 1906, which made every earlier battleship obsolete, and a naval arms race followed.",
 why="Wilhelm II wanted 'a place in the sun' for Germany as a world power, which he believed required a great navy.",
 then="Britain launched the Dreadnought in 1906 and settled its differences with France in 1904 and Russia in 1907.",
 fact="Tirpitz's 'risk theory' held that a fleet two-thirds the size of Britain's would deter it; by August 1914 Britain had 29 dreadnoughts to Germany's 17."),
"eu190483": dict(
 bluf="Britain and France set out to settle the colonial quarrels, over Egypt and Morocco above all, that had brought them close to war at Fashoda in 1898. The agreements of April 1904 traded British control of Egypt for French influence in Morocco. An arrangement that began as colonial bookkeeping became, under German pressure, the core of the coalition that fought Germany in two world wars.",
 now="The Anglo-French partnership forged here fought two world wars together and remains the core of European defence cooperation, including on Ukraine.",
 story="In April 1904 Britain and France signed a set of agreements settling old colonial quarrels: France accepted British control of Egypt, and Britain accepted French influence in Morocco. The agreements carried no military obligation.",
 why="Both feared Germany's growing power and navy. Settling their quarrels over Egypt and Morocco freed them to cooperate.",
 then="Germany tested the Entente in Morocco in 1905 and 1911, which drew Britain and France closer. Russia joined in 1907, forming the Triple Entente.",
 fact="British King Edward VII's state visit to Paris in May 1903, where he won over a hostile crowd, prepared the ground."),
"eu190584": dict(
 bluf="Workers, peasants and liberals pressed Russian Tsar Nicholas II for a constitution after defeat by Japan and the shooting of marchers on Bloody Sunday. He conceded a parliament, the Duma, and civil liberties in the October Manifesto and took much of it back once the army held. The regime survived with its legitimacy spent, and the next crisis, in 1917, finished it.",
 now="Lenin called it the 'dress rehearsal' for 1917: a regime that grants reforms only under pressure and then takes them back can lose its legitimacy.",
 story="On 22 January 1905 troops fired on a peaceful procession led by priest Georgy Gapon to the Winter Palace in St Petersburg; hundreds died. Strikes, peasant risings and a mutiny on the battleship Potemkin spread through the year. In October a general strike forced Russian Tsar Nicholas II to issue the October Manifesto, which granted a parliament, the Duma, and civil liberties.",
 why="Defeat by Japan exposed the regime's incompetence. Industrial workers and peasants lived in deep poverty. The Bloody Sunday massacre destroyed faith in the tsar.",
 then="Nicholas II curtailed the Duma's powers in the Fundamental Laws of 1906 and dissolved the first two Dumas. His prime minister Pyotr Stolypin had more than a thousand rebels executed by field courts-martial, and the anger stayed unresolved.",
 fact="The hangman's noose became known as 'Stolypin's necktie'."),
"d-bleriot": dict(
 bluf="French aviator Louis Blériot flew the Channel to win the Daily Mail's £1,000 prize and prove his monoplane. He crossed from near Calais to Dover in about 37 minutes on 25 July 1909. The sea no longer guaranteed Britain's safety, and the air entered British strategy from then on.",
 now="Air power soon erased Britain's island security, a shift that shaped British strategy in both world wars.",
 story="On 25 July 1909 French aviator Louis Blériot flew his monoplane about 36 km across the English Channel from near Calais to Dover in some 37 minutes, winning the Daily Mail's £1,000 prize. British commentators warned that the country could no longer count on the sea and its navy to keep it safe.",
 why="Newspapers offered prizes to promote aviation. Engines and aircraft were improving fast after the Wright brothers' first flights in 1903.",
 then="Military interest in aircraft grew. Planes fought in World War I five years later.",
 fact="A granite outline of the aircraft marks the landing spot on Northfall Meadow behind Dover Castle."),
"d-christmastruce": dict(
 bluf="British and German soldiers along parts of the Flanders front stopped fighting at Christmas 1914 to bury their dead and, with that done, met in no man's land. The truces held for a day or two and ended when commanders on both sides ordered fire resumed. Armies learned that hostility between conscript soldiers needed discipline and propaganda to maintain, and the war that followed was fought with gas and without such pauses.",
 now="The truce showed commanders that hostility between conscript armies needed discipline and propaganda to maintain, and both sides ordered against repetition; the war's brutalisation followed.",
 story="On Christmas Eve and Christmas Day 1914, along parts of the front, mostly in Belgium, British and German troops called unofficial truces. They buried their dead, swapped food and cigarettes, and in a few places kicked a football about.",
 why="Exhaustion and cold came first. A sense that the enemy was much like themselves came second.",
 then="Commanders forbade fraternising. The war grew more brutal, with poison gas used from April 1915; later truces were rare.",
 fact="German troops set candle-lit Christmas trees on their parapets, and the carol both sides knew was 'Silent Night'."),
"d-marne": dict(
 bluf="German chief of staff Helmuth von Moltke the Younger sought to finish France in six weeks by swinging his right wing round Paris; French commander Joseph Joffre set out to stop it. From 6 September 1914 French and British forces struck into a gap between two German armies and drove them back to the Aisne. Germany lost the short war its whole plan depended on, and four years of trench war followed that broke four empires.",
 now="The Marne stopped Germany short of Paris and destroyed its plan for a short war; the trench stalemate that followed turned the conflict into four years of attrition that broke four empires.",
 story="German armies under chief of staff Helmuth von Moltke the Younger swept through Belgium toward Paris. From 6 September 1914 French forces under commander Joseph Joffre and the British Expeditionary Force counter-attacked into a gap between two German armies; some French reinforcements were rushed from Paris in taxis. The Germans fell back to the Aisne River and dug in.",
 why="Germany's Schlieffen Plan required defeating France in about six weeks. The German right wing had turned east of Paris and exposed its flank.",
 then="Both sides dug trenches from the Channel to Switzerland, and the war became a stalemate.",
 fact="The 'taxis of the Marne' became a French legend of mobilisation."),
"eu191485": dict(
 bluf="Austria-Hungary set out to crush Serbia after Sarajevo with Germany's backing; Russia mobilised to save Serbia; Germany struck France through Belgium to win a two-front war fast; Britain entered to defend Belgium and keep Germany from the Channel. None got the short war each had planned. Four years killed about 20 million people, four empires fell, communism took Russia, and the settlement left the grievances that produced the next war.",
 now="Today's Middle East and Eastern European borders, and the causes of World War II, come from this war. Analysts still study 1914 to understand how a crisis between great powers could spiral into a war nobody wanted.",
 story="On 28 June 1914 Bosnian Serb nationalist Gavrilo Princip assassinated Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to Austria-Hungary, in Sarajevo. Austria-Hungary, backed by Germany, sent Serbia an ultimatum; Russia mobilised to support Serbia; Germany declared war on Russia and France and invaded Belgium, which brought in Britain. Machine guns, artillery and trenches turned the Western Front into a stalemate of mass slaughter.",
 why="Rival alliance blocs, an arms race and mobilisation plans that rewarded striking first came first. Nationalism in the Balkans and among the great powers came second. Austria-Hungary's fear of collapse and Germany's sense of encirclement came third.",
 trig="The assassination in Sarajevo and the July Crisis of ultimatums and mobilisations that followed.",
 then="About 20 million soldiers and civilians died. The German, Austro-Hungarian, Russian and Ottoman empires collapsed; the Bolsheviks took Russia; new states emerged in Eastern Europe; Britain and France divided the Arab Middle East; the United States emerged as the world's creditor.",
 fact="Franz Ferdinand's driver took a wrong turn and stopped right in front of the assassin, Gavrilo Princip."),
"d-lusitania": dict(
 bluf="Germany had declared the waters around Britain a war zone to cut its supplies; the submarine U-20 sank the Cunard liner Lusitania without warning on 7 May 1915. Of 1,959 people aboard, 1,198 died, 128 of them Americans. American opinion turned against Germany, Berlin restricted its submarines until 1917, and the resumption of unrestricted sinking that year brought the United States into the war.",
 now="The sinking made submarine warfare the issue on which American neutrality turned from 1915 to 1917; every later argument over attacks on civilians and neutral opinion starts here.",
 story="On 7 May 1915 the German submarine U-20 torpedoed the British liner Lusitania off southern Ireland without warning, and it sank in 18 minutes. Of 1,959 people aboard, 1,198 died, including 128 Americans. Germany had placed notices in New York newspapers warning that ships in the war zone could be sunk, and the liner was carrying rifle ammunition, which Germany cited to justify the attack.",
 why="Germany had declared the waters around Britain a war zone. The ship was also carrying rifle ammunition.",
 then="American outrage forced Germany to limit submarine attacks until 1917. When it resumed unrestricted warfare in 1917, the United States entered the war.",
 fact="A German medallist struck a satirical medal of the sinking; Britain copied it in large numbers and sold it as propaganda."),
"d-somme": dict(
 bluf="British commander Douglas Haig attacked on the Somme to relieve the French at Verdun and break the German line. The first day cost 57,000 British casualties; by November both sides had lost more than a million men for about 10 km of ground. The British army learned the artillery and infantry methods it used to win in 1918, and the first day became the British image of the whole war.",
 now="The Somme shapes British memory of World War I as futile slaughter and is studied as a lesson in adapting tactics to new technology.",
 story="On 1 July 1916, after a week of bombardment, British troops advanced and were cut down by German machine guns. The battle went on until November, saw the first use of tanks, and gained about 10 km.",
 why="British commander Douglas Haig attacked to relieve pressure on the French at Verdun and to break the German line.",
 then="Commanders learned costly lessons in artillery, infantry tactics and tanks that helped the Allies break the German lines in 1918.",
 fact="The British fired about 1.5 million shells in the opening bombardment; many were duds, and farmers still plough them up every year."),
"d-verdun": dict(
 bluf="German chief of staff Erich von Falkenhayn attacked Verdun to bleed the French army white on ground France would defend at any cost. France held under General Philippe Pétain, and ten months of fighting cost the two sides about 700,000 casualties between them for a front back where it began. Verdun became France's symbol of endurance, made Pétain a national hero and fixed French faith in fixed defences.",
 now="Verdun became the symbol of French endurance and of the futility of attrition; Kohl and Mitterrand chose it in 1984 to stage Franco-German reconciliation.",
 story="From 21 February 1916 German forces attacked the fortress city of Verdun. France rotated most of its army through the battle under General Philippe Pétain and then General Robert Nivelle, with the slogan 'They shall not pass'. By December the front was back near where it started.",
 why="German chief of staff Erich von Falkenhayn wanted to wear down France's army on ground it could never give up.",
 then="Verdun made Pétain a national hero, which later helped him lead Vichy France. France built the Maginot Line in the 1930s on the lesson of fixed defence.",
 fact="Fort Douaumont, the strongest fort at Verdun, fell on 25 February to a small German party that found it almost unmanned."),
"d-february": dict(
 bluf="Petrograd workers struck for bread and the garrison refused to fire on them; Russian Tsar Nicholas II's generals told him to go. He abdicated on 15 March 1917, and three centuries of Romanov rule ended in a week. Power split between a Provisional Government and the soviets, and the government's choice to fight on gave the Bolsheviks their opening.",
 now="It ended more than 300 years of Romanov rule, and the failure of the new government to end the war or build stable institutions opened the way for the Bolsheviks.",
 story="Strikes and food protests began in Petrograd on International Women's Day, 8 March 1917, 23 February in Russia's calendar. When soldiers joined the crowds, Russian Tsar Nicholas II's generals advised him to go, and he abdicated on 15 March. A Provisional Government took over, alongside workers' councils called soviets.",
 why="Millions of war dead and food shortages in the cities came first. A monarchy discredited by defeat and by Rasputin's influence came second. When the capital's garrison refused to fire, the regime fell.",
 then="The Provisional Government kept fighting the war. The Bolsheviks seized power in November.",
 fact="Nicholas abdicated for his son as well as himself; his brother Michael refused the crown the next day."),
"eu191786": dict(
 bluf="Petrograd's workers and soldiers brought down the tsar in March 1917; Bolshevik leader Vladimir Lenin then set out to take power from the Provisional Government that fought on. The Bolsheviks seized the capital in November, shut the elected assembly and won a civil war. The first communist state produced the century's central ideological conflict, inspired revolutionaries worldwide and became a one-party dictatorship that killed millions.",
 now="The 20th century's great ideological struggle, communism against capitalism, starts here, and Russia's later authoritarianism draws on Soviet habits of rule.",
 story="In February 1917, March in the Western calendar, strikes and bread riots in Petrograd, joined by soldiers, forced Russian Tsar Nicholas II to abdicate. A Provisional Government kept fighting the war while workers' and soldiers' councils, the soviets, competed for power. In October, November in the West, Bolshevik leader Vladimir Lenin's party seized the capital promising 'peace, land and bread', shut down the elected Constituent Assembly and fought a civil war against its opponents.",
 why="War losses, food shortages and a discredited autocracy came first. Peasant hunger for land came second. The Provisional Government's decision to stay in the war came third.",
 trig="Bread riots and strikes in Petrograd in February 1917; in October, the collapse of the Provisional Government's authority and Lenin's decision to seize power.",
 then="The Bolsheviks made peace with Germany, won the civil war and founded the USSR in 1922. Under Stalin the state collectivised agriculture, industrialised by force and imprisoned and killed millions.",
 fact="The 'October Revolution' happened in November by the Western calendar; Russia still used the old Julian calendar."),
"na191761": dict(
 bluf="US President Woodrow Wilson asked Congress for war to stop German submarine attacks on American ships and, as he put it, to make the world safe for democracy. Two million American troops and American credit tipped the war to the Allies by late 1918. The United States arrived as a decisive power and then rejected the League of Nations, and the argument between engagement and isolation has run through its foreign policy since.",
 now="American entry decided the war and set the pattern of 1917 to 1945: a United States that intervenes decisively and then argues over whether it should have.",
 story="Germany resumed unrestricted submarine warfare on 1 February 1917 and sank American ships. The Zimmermann Telegram, published in March, showed Germany offering Mexico US territory for joining its side. US President Woodrow Wilson asked Congress for war on 2 April, and Congress declared it on 6 April 1917.",
 why="Germany resumed unrestricted submarine warfare in February 1917 and sank American ships. The Zimmermann Telegram revealed it had offered Mexico US territory. The United States also had heavy loans to Britain and France at stake.",
 then="Two million US troops reached France and helped force Germany to seek an armistice.",
 fact="The Zimmermann Telegram was intercepted and decoded by British naval intelligence, which passed it to Washington."),
"d-brestlitovsk": dict(
 bluf="Bolshevik leader Vladimir Lenin wanted peace at any price to hold power with an army that had dissolved; Germany wanted its eastern troops free for the west. The treaty of 3 March 1918 gave up Poland, the Baltic lands, Finland and Ukraine, with about a third of Russia's population and much of its industry and farmland. Germany got a short-lived eastern empire, and the Bolsheviks showed they would trade land for power.",
 now="Several of the lost lands, from Finland and the Baltic states to Poland, became independent, and Russia's view of them as lost borderlands still shapes its policy.",
 story="Signed on 3 March 1918, the treaty took Russia out of World War I. Russia gave up Poland, the Baltic lands, Finland and Ukraine, with about a third of its population and much of its industry and farmland.",
 why="The Bolsheviks needed peace to hold on to power. The army had collapsed.",
 then="Germany moved troops west for a last offensive. The treaty was cancelled after Germany's defeat in November.",
 fact="Soviet Foreign Commissar Leon Trotsky's first answer at the talks was 'neither war nor peace'; the Germans resumed their advance and the Bolsheviks signed worse terms."),
"d-rcw": dict(
 bluf="The Bolsheviks fought to hold the state they had seized; White generals, national movements and Allied expeditions fought to remove them. Three years of war, terror on both sides, famine and disease killed perhaps 7 to 12 million people and ended in Bolshevik victory. The Soviet state came out militarised and one-party, with a memory of foreign intervention it has invoked since.",
 now="The war shaped the Soviet state's militarised, one-party character, and the Allied intervention fed a Russian narrative of Western hostility that is still invoked.",
 story="From 1918, anti-Bolshevik White armies, nationalist forces and Allied troops fought the Red Army built by War Commissar Leon Trotsky. Fighting, terror on both sides, famine and disease killed perhaps 7 to 12 million people.",
 why="The Bolsheviks' seizure of power came first. Their closing of the elected Constituent Assembly came second.",
 then="The Bolsheviks won through central control, terror and their enemies' disunity, and founded the Soviet Union in 1922.",
 fact="The Czechoslovak Legion, some 40,000 former prisoners of war trying to leave Russia by the Trans-Siberian Railway, seized the line in 1918 and opened the war in Siberia."),
"d-romanovs": dict(
 bluf="The Ural Regional Soviet, with Moscow's approval, shot Russian Tsar Nicholas II and his family to deny the approaching Whites a rallying point. Eleven people died in the cellar of the Ipatiev House on the night of 16-17 July 1918. The killing marked the Bolsheviks' readiness to use terror, and the family's canonisation in 2000 marked post-Soviet Russia's recovery of its tsarist past.",
 now="The Russian Orthodox Church canonised the family in 2000, part of post-Soviet Russia's embrace of its tsarist past alongside its Soviet one.",
 story="With White forces approaching Yekaterinburg, the Ural Regional Soviet, with Moscow's approval, had Russian Tsar Nicholas II, Empress Alexandra, their five children and four servants shot in the night of 16-17 July 1918. Their bodies were hidden; most were found in 1979 and identified by DNA in the 1990s, the last two in 2007.",
 why="The Bolsheviks feared the Whites would free the tsar and rally around him.",
 then="Rumours that a daughter, Anastasia, had survived lasted for decades until DNA disproved them.",
 fact="The remains were reburied in the Peter and Paul Cathedral in St Petersburg in July 1998, with Russian President Boris Yeltsin present."),
"d-spanishflu": dict(
 bluf="A new influenza strain spread with the troop movements of 1918 in waves, the second of them lethal to healthy young adults. It killed between about 17 and 50 million people or more, more than the war itself. Governments built up their public health services, and the pandemic remains the benchmark for pandemic planning.",
 now="It remains the benchmark for pandemic planning; scientists reconstructed the virus in 2005 to study how pandemics spread.",
 story="Influenza spread with troops and ships in 1918, in waves. The deadliest wave struck almost at once in August and September at Brest in France, Freetown in Sierra Leone and Boston. It killed many healthy young adults; estimates of the dead range from about 17 to 50 million or more.",
 why="Crowded camps, troop ships and wartime hunger helped a new flu virus spread.",
 then="It faded by 1920. Many countries built up their public health services.",
 fact="It was called 'Spanish' only because neutral Spain's newspapers reported it freely while warring countries censored the news."),
"eu191887": dict(
 bluf="Germany's high command sought an armistice before the army broke and before revolution at home finished the state; the Allies set terms that made resumption impossible. The guns stopped at 11 am on 11 November 1918 with the German army still on foreign soil. Four empires fell, and the way the war ended let German nationalists claim the army had been stabbed in the back at home.",
 now="An armistice signed with the army still abroad let German nationalists claim from 1919 that it had been stabbed in the back at home; the claim carried the Nazi case against the republic.",
 story="With its army retreating, its allies collapsing and revolution breaking out at home, Germany sent delegates to meet Allied commander Ferdinand Foch in a railway carriage in the forest of Compiègne. The armistice took effect at 11 am on 11 November 1918.",
 why="Germany was exhausted and its allies had collapsed. Revolution was breaking out at home.",
 then="The German, Austro-Hungarian, Russian and Ottoman empires fell. New countries followed, among them Poland, Czechoslovakia and Yugoslavia.",
 myth="Germany's army was undefeated in the field. Its high command asked for an armistice in October because the front was giving way under the Allied offensives of August to October.",
 fact="Remembrance Day at 11 am on 11 November marks the hour the guns stopped."),
"eu191988": dict(
 bluf="French Premier Georges Clemenceau wanted security against Germany, British Prime Minister David Lloyd George wanted its navy and colonies gone, and US President Woodrow Wilson wanted a League of Nations. The treaty they signed on 28 June 1919 blamed Germany for the war, took its colonies and about 13 per cent of its European territory, capped its army at 100,000 and set reparations. Harsh enough to be hated and too weakly enforced to hold, it fixed German politics on revision for twenty years.",
 now="The treaty's terms fixed German politics for twenty years: every party demanded revision, the republic that signed it carried the blame, and the settlement was harsh enough to be hated and too weakly enforced to prevent rearmament. The 1945 settlement was designed as its opposite.",
 story="Signed on 28 June 1919 in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles, the treaty blamed Germany for the war, took its colonies and about 13 per cent of its European territory, limited its army to 100,000 men and required reparations later set at 132 billion gold marks.",
 why="France, invaded twice in 50 years, wanted security and reparations. Britain wanted German naval and colonial power curbed. US President Woodrow Wilson wanted a League of Nations and self-determination. The compromise satisfied no one.",
 then="Germans called it a 'dictated peace'. The US Senate rejected it, reparations were repeatedly rescheduled and largely unpaid, and Britain and France failed to enforce its limits when Hitler rearmed.",
 fact="It was signed in the Hall of Mirrors, where Germany had proclaimed its empire in 1871, on the fifth anniversary of Franz Ferdinand's assassination."),
"d-league": dict(
 bluf="US President Woodrow Wilson wanted a permanent body in which open diplomacy and collective security replaced the balance of power. The League came into being in January 1920 with 42 members and without the United States. It settled small disputes and ran mandates and refugee work, failed against Japan, Italy and Germany in the 1930s, and its failures shaped the design of the United Nations.",
 now="The United Nations replaced it in 1945-46, with a Security Council where the great powers hold vetoes, designed to keep them inside the system that the League had lost.",
 story="Created by the Treaty of Versailles, the League came into being on 10 January 1920. It settled some small disputes and ran mandates and refugee work. The US never joined, and the League could not stop Japan, Italy or Germany in the 1930s.",
 why="Horror at the slaughter of World War I came first. US President Woodrow Wilson's belief that open diplomacy and collective security could replace the balance of power came second.",
 then="Japan and Germany left in 1933 and Italy in 1937, and the League could not stop the aggressions that led to World War II.",
 fact="The United States, whose president designed it, never joined; the Senate rejected the treaty in November 1919 and again in March 1920."),
"eu192289": dict(
 bluf="Fascist leader Benito Mussolini set out to take power by the threat of force while leaving the king a legal way to hand it over. Italian King Victor Emmanuel III refused martial law and made him prime minister as the Blackshirts gathered outside Rome in October 1922. Italy became the first fascist state by 1925, and Hitler and authoritarian movements worldwide took it as a model.",
 now="Fascism remains the benchmark for movements that use democratic institutions to win power and then dismantle them, and arguments over the label run through politics today.",
 story="As thousands of Fascist Blackshirts converged on Rome on 28 October 1922, Italian King Victor Emmanuel III refused to declare martial law and asked Fascist leader Benito Mussolini to become prime minister. By 1925 Mussolini had made Italy a one-party dictatorship.",
 why="Post-war strikes and fear of socialist revolution came first. Anger at the 'mutilated victory' of 1919 came second.",
 then="Italy became the first fascist state: other parties banned, the press controlled, opponents jailed or killed.",
 fact="Mussolini travelled to Rome by sleeper train from Milan after the king's summons; the march entered the city after he was already prime minister."),
"eu192289b": dict(
 bluf="Bolshevik leader Vladimir Lenin wanted the lands of the old empire bound together under the Communist Party in a form that looked like a free union. The Union of Soviet Socialist Republics was declared on 30 December 1922 as a federation of Russia, Ukraine, Belarus and Transcaucasia ruled from Moscow. The internal borders drawn then became the frontiers of fifteen states in 1991.",
 now="The USSR's internal borders became the borders of 15 independent countries in 1991.",
 story="After winning the civil war, the Bolsheviks joined Russia with Ukraine, Belarus and the Transcaucasian republic in the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics on 30 December 1922. It was formally a federation and in practice ruled by the Communist Party from Moscow.",
 why="The Bolsheviks wanted the former Russian Empire's lands bound together under Communist rule.",
 then="Lenin died in 1924. Joseph Stalin, who controlled the party machine, won the struggle to succeed him.",
 fact="Lenin, already ill, had fought Stalin over the terms of the union and attacked his 'Great Russian chauvinism' in notes dictated that December."),
"d-beerhall": dict(
 bluf="Nazi leader Adolf Hitler, with General Erich Ludendorff, tried to seize Bavaria and march on Berlin as Mussolini had marched on Rome. Police broke the march in Munich on 9 November 1923; 16 Nazis and 4 policemen died, and Hitler got five years and served about nine months. He came out a national figure with a book and a new strategy, to win power through elections, which worked.",
 now="The lenient sentence is often cited as a failure of a democracy to defend itself, and debates over punishing coup attempts still invoke it.",
 story="On 8 November 1923 Nazi leader Adolf Hitler burst into a Munich beer hall and proclaimed a national revolution. The next day police fired on his marchers; 16 Nazis and 4 policemen died. Sentenced to five years, Hitler served about nine months, writing 'Mein Kampf'.",
 why="Hitler hoped to copy Mussolini's March on Rome in the chaos of hyperinflation.",
 then="Hitler decided to win power through elections instead. The Nazis turned the dead of the putsch into martyrs for their propaganda.",
 fact="The judge let Hitler turn the trial into a speech, and newspapers across Germany reported it."),
"d-hyperinflation": dict(
 bluf="The Weimar government printed money to pay its bills, meet reparations and support workers striking against the French occupation of the Ruhr. Prices lost all meaning by the autumn of 1923, and middle-class savings vanished. The Rentenmark ended it in November, and a German fear of inflation that shaped policy and the design of the European Central Bank dates from it.",
 now="Fear of inflation still shapes German economic policy and its insistence on an independent central bank, a principle built into the European Central Bank.",
 story="Printing money to pay its bills and to support workers resisting the French occupation of the Ruhr, the German government lost control of prices. Prices doubled every few days, workers were paid twice a day, and savings were wiped out. By November 1923 one US dollar cost 4.2 trillion marks.",
 why="Germany had financed the war with debt. It then printed money to pay its bills and reparations and to support workers on strike against the French occupation of the Ruhr.",
 then="A new currency, the Rentenmark, ended it in late 1923. The middle class kept the memory.",
 fact="Workers spent their pay at midday before prices rose again, and banknotes were used as wallpaper and fuel."),
"eu192490": dict(
 bluf="Joseph Stalin set out to succeed Lenin by controlling the party's appointments rather than winning its arguments. By 1929 he had used the General Secretary's office to defeat Leon Trotsky and every other rival. He turned the Soviet Union into a personal dictatorship that industrialised by force at the cost of famine, terror and millions of deaths, and built the state that beat Germany in 1945.",
 now="Russia's ambivalence about Stalin, as victor in World War II and as mass murderer, and Ukraine's memory of the Holodomor are both rooted in his rule.",
 story="After Lenin died in January 1924, Joseph Stalin used his post as the party's General Secretary to fill it with loyalists. He allied with rivals to defeat Leon Trotsky, then turned on them; by 1929 he was undisputed leader.",
 why="As General Secretary, Stalin controlled party appointments and outmanoeuvred more famous rivals.",
 then="Forced collectivisation, famine and purges killed millions. The USSR also industrialised fast.",
 fact="Lenin's last testament asked the party to remove Stalin as General Secretary; it was read to delegates and then suppressed."),
"d-lindbergh": dict(
 bluf="American aviator Charles Lindbergh flew alone from New York to Paris to win the $25,000 Orteig Prize for the first non-stop flight between the two cities. He landed at Le Bourget on 21 May 1927 after 33 and a half hours before a crowd of tens of thousands. Aviation became a mass enthusiasm, investment poured into airlines, and the oceans stopped guaranteeing distance between continents.",
 now="Lindbergh later led the 'America First' campaign against entering World War II, making him a symbol of America's prewar debate over intervention.",
 story="American aviator Charles Lindbergh took off from Roosevelt Field on 20 May 1927 in the Spirit of St. Louis and landed at Le Bourget the next evening before a crowd of tens of thousands. He won a $25,000 prize.",
 why="Hotelier Raymond Orteig had offered a prize for the first non-stop flight between New York and Paris.",
 then="Interest in flying boomed, investment in aviation surged, and airlines began carrying passengers across the United States.",
 fact="Lindbergh carried no radio and no parachute, and saw ahead through a periscope because the fuel tank blocked the windscreen."),
}
